<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chełmno</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kulm, Culm</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chełmno, German Kulm, is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in the Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Granted a charter by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1233, it gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-law">Chełmno law</a> used by hundreds of towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and Poland, was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, and passed to Poland in 1466. Its walled medieval centre survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chelmno.jpg" alt="Chełmno" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kazimierz Mendlik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Che%C5%82mno_-_panoramio_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chełmno is a town near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Its name comes from chelm, an old Polish word for hill. Because of its regional importance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it gave its name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a>, to the local Catholic diocese and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-law">Kulm law</a>, used by over 180 cities and towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a>. Its population was mixed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Germans, with Poles two thirds in the second half of the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first written mention is in a document allegedly issued in 1065. In 1226 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a> invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a>, and in 1233 the town received the city rights known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-law">Kulm law</a>, renewed in 1251. In 1243 it became the nominal see of the Diocese of Chełmno, though the cathedral was at neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmza">Chełmża</a>. A member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, it grew prosperous. In 1440 it was a founder of the Prussian Confederation against the Order's rule, and after the Thirteen Years' War the Order recognised it as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. In 1692 the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> became the Chełmno Academy.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed the town in 1772 and called it Kulm. The Prussians expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> professors, abolished the Polish academy and closed Catholic monasteries. The surgeon Ludwik Rydygier opened a clinic in 1878 and performed pioneering operations. Polish troops entered the town on 22 January 1920. In 1939 the German authorities murdered 5,000 Polish civilians in the territory, and the rest of the Polish population was expelled to the General Government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chełmno keeps a well-preserved medieval centre with five Gothic churches, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> town hall in the market square, rebuilt in 1567–1572, and city walls surviving almost whole. The Old Town was designated one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s official Historic Monuments on 20 April 2005.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chełmno” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In occupied Europe, mobile killing units of the Einsatzgruppen shot Jews in the Soviet territories from 1941, and the Wannsee Conference of January 1942 coordinated the deportation of Jews from across the continent to extermination camps in occupied Poland: <b>Chełmno</b>, Bełżec, Sobibór, Treblinka, Majdanek and Auschwitz-Birkenau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The province took in Pomerelia with the mouth of the Vistula and the city of Danzig, the <b>Chełmno</b> and Michałów Lands with Thorn, the Prince-Bishopric of Warmia with Frauenburg, Heilsberg and Allenstein, and the Marienburger Land with Marienburg and the city of Elbing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 1230 the Landmeister Hermann Balk led the knights across the Vistula, founding Thorn in 1231, <b>Kulm</b> in 1232 and Elbing in 1237.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1243 the papal legate William of Modena divided conquered Prussia into four dioceses: <b>Culm</b>, Pomesania, Warmia and Samland.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-culm">Bishopric of Culm</a></li></ul></section>
